`,ana:"#material.inorganic.stone.sedimentary_rock.limestone.calcite_limestone.red.calcare-lucidabile_rosso_sicilia-orientale",type:"stone.limestone.polishable.red",subtype:["calcare_rosso_SiciliaOR_taormina"],resp:"#Coccato",ref:"http://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/material/lod/66.html",placeName:{type:"provenance",ref:{_:"Taormina",target:"https://www.geonames.org/2522975/taormina.html"},location:{geo:"37.85358,15.28851",precision:{match:"preceding-sibling::geo",n:"5000"}}},note:{_:" Petrographic imagery at ",ref:{_:"https://iiif.csad.ox.ac.uk/viewer/isicily/ISic003125",target:"https://iiif.csad.ox.ac.uk/viewer/isicily/ISic003125"}}},condition:{ana:"#condition.complete"},layoutDesc:{layout:{p:"Four lines of Greek letters of equal height, centred on the upper two thirds of the front face of the base.",rs:{_:"chiselled",ana:"#execution.chiselled",ref:"http://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/writing/lod/1"},damage:{ana:"#text_condition.complete"}}},handNote:{lettering:["Deeply incised letters with a regular module (although with a little bit of cramping in lines 2 and 3) and of even height throughout. Terminal serifs of a simple wedge form. Alpha is consistently pronounced broken bar; epsilon has shorter middle bar, only lightly connected, with slight overlap of the top and bottom bar; theta is a full circle, with a short detached mid-stroke; kappa has shorter arms (especially the lower arm); mu has vertical outer strokes, with the middle strokes extending three-quarters of the way down to the base line; omicron is full size; pi has slightly overlapping crossbar, with equal length verticals; rho a large rounded eye; sigma has parallel top and bottom strokes, with a slight extension at the join of the mid-strokes; phi has small eye with slightly extended vertical; omega is slightly smaller than the other letters, tear-drop shaped, joined to the base line (but the eye does not close before the baseline) ",{_:`
